s": ["For children who dislike getting wet, let them build a dry block-tower 'viewing deck' next to the water for their toy.", "For extra sensory fun, add bubble bath to the water tray for a soapy splash park."], "Preparation": ["Fill the water tray halfway with warm water.", "Gather a basket of plastic blocks, cups, and toy animals next to the tray."], "MaterialSetup": ["Drop a few cups and toys directly in the water to invite immediate play.", "Keep blocks and sponges dry in baskets nearby so kids can choose when to wet them."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Place the water tray in an easy-to-reach spot.", "Lay down towels or a plastic splash mat underneath to catch wild splashes."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Oh no! Our toy animal friends are so hot and want to go to a wild water park! But there are no rides yet! Can you help me build the silliest splash islands, slides, and pools ever? Let's make some waves!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child naturally starts making their toy talk or slide down a block, lean in and pretend to be a customer waiting in line for their amazing ride!", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy light and joyful. Act as the play coordinator—narrate the toys' adventures with fun sound effects (Wheee! Splash!) and encourage silly ways to move water, like pouring from high up or making mini waves.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	df5d8828-f7cb-4250-9cd1-dba1d787d531	2026-10-07 23:10:39.882758+00
