57c06b1a-a342-4160-ba17-187de55c71a9	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	3	Leaping Lily Pad Math Quest	Children decorate pre-folded leaping origami paper frogs and practice finger pressure control to make them leap across numbered lily pads.	{"AgeMax": 4, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "A custom origami frog and a lily pad target course marked with numbers and colors.", "Together": "We decorate our paper frogs with colorful patterns and test how pressing the back of the frog makes it launch into the air.", "Challenge": "Can you make your paper frog jump from lily pad to lily pad to reach the big pond?", "Materials": ["Pre-cut square paper (green, yellow, light blue)", "Pre-folded paper jumping frogs", "Green foam or felt lily pads cutouts with numbers 1-5", "Washable markers", "Googly eyes and glue dots", "Large blue floor mat or blue fabric sheet"], "Reflection": "How did your frog move differently when you pressed softly compared to when you pressed hard?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their frog color, decorative stickers, and which numbered path to leap along.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exciting instant the paper frog launches into the air and lands right on a green lily pad.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Mastering the finger tap technique to send the paper frog leaping accurately onto a target.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What happens when you press the back of the frog very quickly? Which lily pad is your frog aiming for next?", "Try": "If a child struggles to make the frog leap, place your finger over theirs to guide the downward slide release motion.", "Watch": "Observe how children adjust their finger pressure and fine motor precision when trying to launch the frog.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Frog Personalization", "ChildrenDo": "Decorate pre-folded paper frogs using markers, dots, and googly eyes.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrate how to stick googly eyes on the front and praise unique color choices.", "DurationMinutes": 8}, {"Name": "Launch Practice", "ChildrenDo": "Practice pressing down on the rear spring fold of the frog using one finger to make it launch.", "TeacherDoes": "Guide children's finger placement on the back fold. Model light versus firm pressure.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Lily Pad Hop Game", "ChildrenDo": "Launch frogs along the numbered lily pad paths, calling out the numbers as frogs land on them.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to aim for specific numbers and celebrate every successful jump.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "Tidy & Group Reflection", "ChildrenDo": "Gather paper frogs and collect lily pads into the storage basket.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead a brief cool-down transition chant about sleepy frogs resting in the pond.", "DurationMinutes": 2}], "Safety": ["Ensure children keep small googly eyes away from their mouths.", "Keep the jumping area clear of foot traffic to prevent tripping."], "Closing": "Place all the paper frogs together on the large center lily pad for a quick group photo before transition.", "Extensions": ["Add a tape measure to compare which frog jumped the longest distance.", "Introduce addition by adding up the numbers on the lily pads where the frog lands."], "Adaptations": ["Provide larger pre-folded frogs made of thick paper for children needing bigger motor targets.", "Draw visual directional arrows on the lily pads to assist visual learners."], "Preparation": ["Pre-fold 2-3 origami jumping frogs per child using sturdy cardstock or origami paper.", "Cut out 10-15 green felt circles to serve as lily pads and write numbers 1 to 5 on them.", "Spread the blue fabric sheet on the floor to represent the pond."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place blank origami frogs, markers, googly eyes, and glue dots at tables.", "Set the pre-folded blank frogs in small trays for easy access."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a wide floor space around the blue fabric pond.", "Arrange lily pads in linear paths leading toward the center of the pond."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Look at these amazing paper frogs! When we press down gently on their backs, they hop high into the air. Let's make our own frogs and help them leap across the lily pads!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "A quiet moment occurs when a child gently places their finger on the frog, takes a focused breath, and watches it flip successfully.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Focus on the physical mechanics of pressing down on the spring-fold. Pre-fold the frogs so children can focus on decorating and playing without frustration.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-12 12:03:48.114101+00
eb3c7f91-97dd-416e-9df9-3649b2792a0b	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	4	Leaping Lily Pad Math Quest	Children decorate pre-folded leaping origami paper frogs and practice finger pressure control to make them leap across numbered lily pads.	{"AgeMax": 4, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "A custom origami frog and a lily pad target course marked with numbers and colors.", "Together": "We decorate our paper frogs with colorful patterns and test how pressing the back of the frog makes it launch into the air.", "Challenge": "Can you make your paper frog jump from lily pad to lily pad to reach the big pond?", "Materials": ["Pre-cut square paper (green, yellow, light blue)", "Pre-folded paper jumping frogs", "Green foam or felt lily pads cutouts with numbers 1-5", "Washable markers", "Googly eyes and glue dots", "Large blue floor mat or blue fabric sheet"], "Reflection": "How did your frog move differently when you pressed softly compared to when you pressed hard?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their frog color, decorative stickers, and which numbered path to leap along.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exciting instant the paper frog launches into the air and lands right on a green lily pad.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Mastering the finger tap technique to send the paper frog leaping accurately onto a target.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What happens when you press the back of the frog very quickly? Which lily pad is your frog aiming for next?", "Try": "If a child struggles to make the frog leap, place your finger over theirs to guide the downward slide release motion.", "Watch": "Observe how children adjust their finger pressure and fine motor precision when trying to launch the frog.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Frog Personalization", "ChildrenDo": "Decorate pre-folded paper frogs using markers, dots, and googly eyes.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrate how to stick googly eyes on the front and praise unique color choices.", "Dura