8f41b082-4675-4a1f-bf13-ef1f0b6d984d	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	3	Tiny Discoveries	Look closely at tiny things hiding in the classroom.	{"AgeMax": 3, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "", "Together": "", "Challenge": "", "Materials": [], "Reflection": "", "ChildChoice": "", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "", "Try": "", "Watch": "", "Phases": [], "Safety": [], "Closing": "", "Extensions": [], "Adaptations": [], "Preparation": [], "MaterialSetup": [], "EnvironmentSetup": [], "TeacherIntroduction": "", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:05:55.21199+00
e2b4b761-6d98-4fcf-af6a-d8c9edc70718	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	4	Exploring Kids Finding Little Star And Placing It In A Corresponding Pallet: Creative Tactile Studio	Children engage in open-ended creative construction and tactile exploration centered on Kids finding little star and placing it in a corresponding pallet.	{"AgeMax": 3, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "A tangible collaborative model or collage.", "Together": "Children share building materials and describe what they are making to their peers.", "Challenge": "Can we design and build a display inspired by Kids finding little star and placing it in a corresponding pallet?", "Materials": ["Cardstock", "Washable craft adhesive", "Wooden craft sticks", "Textured paper scraps"], "Reflection": "What was your favorite texture or tool you used while exploring Kids finding little star and placing it in a corresponding pallet?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose which textures, shapes, and tools they wish to combine.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "Noticing how different materials join together to form a new creation.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Presenting their completed project during reflection circle.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "How does that piece feel when you touch it?", "Try": "Try connecting two different shapes together.", "Watch": "Observe hand-eye coordination and peer turn-taking.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Welcome & Material Touch", "ChildrenDo": "Feel materials and select initial pieces", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrates gentle handling and asks open questions", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Hands-On Creation", "ChildrenDo": "Assemble and combine elements", "TeacherDoes": "Circulates, providing positive reinforcement", "DurationMinutes": 15}, {"Name": "Sharing & Clean-Up", "ChildrenDo": "Place finished crafts on drying rack and tidy up", "TeacherDoes": "Leads a gentle closing transition", "DurationMinutes": 5}], "Safety": ["Ensure only child-safe non-toxic adhesive is used", "Remind children that materials remain on the table"], "Closing": "Gather on the carpet for a quick proud celebration of everyone's work.", "Extensions": ["Take photos of finished structures to create a classroom storybook"], "Adaptations": ["Provide larger chunky pieces for developing fine-motor grip"], "Preparation": ["Cut paper into varied shapes", "Set out trays on low work tables"], "MaterialSetup": ["Place trays evenly within reach of small groups"], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Spacious floor or craft table with accessible trays"], "TeacherIntroduction": "Today we are exploring Kids finding little star and placing it in a corresponding pallet! Look at all these interesting textures we can build with.", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "Praise a child who noticed an interesting detail in their friend's project.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Introduce materials at the low table. Encourage children to test how pieces fit together.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:06:05.627112+00
6debcf2a-2635-42df-96ad-8641cebec4cb	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	5			{"AgeMax": 3, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "", "Together": "", "Challenge": "", "Materials": [], "Reflection": "", "ChildChoice": "", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "", "Try": "", "Watch": "", "Phases": [], "Safety": [], "Closing": "", "Extensions": [], "Adaptations": [], "Preparation": [], "MaterialSetup": [], "EnvironmentSetup": [], "TeacherIntroduction": "", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:06:16.693207+00
22574801-20ad-4312-8fbf-ca0a6d230240	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002	1			{"AgeMax": 5, "AgeMin": 3, "Create": "", "Together": "", "Challenge": "", "Materials": [], "Reflection": "", "ChildChoice": "", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "", "Try": "", "Watch": "", "Phases": [], "Safety": [], "Closing": "", "Extensions": [], "Adaptations": [], "Preparation": [], "MaterialSetup": [], "EnvironmentSetup": [], "TeacherIntroduction": "", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:07:16.719937+00
b9d5355f-fb3e-43d4-afd7-df71ec035b6a	60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003	6	Leaping Lily Pad Math Quest (Focus: Make It More Playful)	Children decorate pre-folded leaping origami paper frogs and practice finger pressure control to make them leap across numbered lily pads. Adapted with emphasis on: Make it more playful.	{"AgeMax": 4, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "A custom origami frog and a lily pad target course marked with numbers and colors.", "Together": "We decorate our paper frogs with colorful patterns and test how pressing the back of the frog makes it launch into the air.", "Challenge": "Can you make your paper frog jump from lily pad to lily pad to reach the big pond? Challenge adaptation: Make it more playful.", "Materials": ["Pre-cut square paper (green, yellow, light blue)", "Pre-folded paper jumping frogs", "Green foam or felt lily pads cutouts with numbers 1-5", "Washable markers", "Googly eyes and glue dots", "Large blue floor mat or blue fabric sheet"], "Reflection": "How did your frog move differently when you pressed softly compared to when you pressed hard?", "ChildChoice": "Children choose their frog color, decorative stickers, and which numbered path to leap along.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "The exciting instant the paper frog launches into the air and lands right on a green lily pad.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Mastering the finger tap technique to send the paper frog leaping accurately onto a target.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What happens when you press the back of the frog very quickly? Which lily pad is your frog aiming for next?", "Try": "If a child struggles to make the frog leap, place your finger over theirs to guide the downward slide release motion.", "Watch": "Observe how children adjust their finger pressure and fine motor precision when trying to launch the frog.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Frog Personalization", "ChildrenDo": "Decorate pre-folded paper frogs using markers, dots, and googly eyes.", "TeacherDoes": "Demonstrate how to stick googly eyes on the front and praise unique color choices.", "DurationMinutes": 8}, {"Name": "Launch Practice", "ChildrenDo": "Practice pressing down on the rear spring fold of the frog using one finger to make it launch.", "TeacherDoes": "Guide children's finger placement on the back fold. Model light versus firm pressure.", "DurationMinutes": 5}, {"Name": "Lily Pad Hop Game", "ChildrenDo": "Launch frogs along the numbered lily pad paths, calling out the numbers as frogs land on them.", "TeacherDoes": "Encourage children to aim for specific numbers and celebrate every successful jump.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "Tidy & Group Reflection", "ChildrenDo": "Gather paper frogs and collect lily pads into the storage basket.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead a brief cool-down transition chant about sleepy frogs resting in the pond.", "DurationMinutes": 2}], "Safety": ["Ensure children keep small googly eyes away from their mouths.", "Keep the jumping area clear of foot traffic to prevent tripping."], "Closing": "Place all the paper frogs together on the large center lily pad for a quick group photo before transition.", "Extensions": ["Add a tape measure to compare which frog jumped the longest distance.", "Introduce addition by adding up the numbers on the lily pads where the frog lands."], "Adaptations": ["Provide larger pre-folded frogs made of thick paper for children needing bigger motor targets.", "Draw visual directional arrows on the lily pads to assist visual learners."], "Preparation": ["Pre-fold 2-3 origami jumping frogs per child using sturdy cardstock or origami paper.", "Cut out 10-15 green felt circles to serve as lily pads and write numbers 1 to 5 on them.", "Spread the blue fabric sheet on the floor to represent the pond."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place blank origami frogs, markers, googly eyes, and glue dots at tables.", "Set the pre-folded blank frogs in small trays for easy access."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Clear a wide floor space around the blue fabric pond.", "Arrange lily pads in linear paths leading toward the center of the pond."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Look at these amazing paper frogs! When we press down gently on their backs, they hop high into the air. Let's make our own frogs and help them leap across the lily pads!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "A quiet moment occurs when a child gently places their finger on the frog, takes a focused breath, and watches it flip successfully.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Focus on the physical mechanics of pressing down on the spring-fold. Pre-fold the frogs so children can focus on decorating and playing without frustration.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:17:47.520163+00
e24b5d5c-a786-4c3b-a43e-7d34cf45e9d4	a7409952-18ab-47e0-a7ab-a5b53426343f	8	The Wiggle-Wobble Clay Monster Party (Focus: Make It More Playful)	Children wake up sleeping lumps of clay with silly tickles and splats, then transform them into goofy, wiggly monster friends using shaky googly eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and fluffy tickle-feathers. Adapted with emphasis on: Make it more playful.	{"AgeMax": 3, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "Pinch a goofy nose, press in tons of googly eyes, add bouncy zig-zag pipe cleaner legs, and give your monster a fluffy feather tail for tickling.", "Together": "Let's pat, squeeze, and gently splat our clay on the table! We'll tickle the clay to 'wake it up' and make funny yawning noises together.", "Challenge": "Can you make a super silly, wiggly clay monster that loves to dance and make funny noises? Challenge adaptation: Make it more playful.", "Materials": ["Brightly colored playdough or air-dry clay", "Assorted sizes of googly eyes", "Colorful pipe cleaners (some pre-bent into zig-zags)", "Fluffy craft feathers", "Soft mini pom-poms for silly noses", "Small plastic cups (to hide the craft parts under)"], "Reflection": "Show me your monster's favorite dance move! What silly sound does it make when you tickle its nose?", "ChildChoice": "Deciding how many eyes to stack, bending pipe cleaners into wild shapes, and inventing their monster's signature silly voice and dance move.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "When a child tickles their finished monster's tummy with a feather and lets out a huge, shared giggle as they act out the monster's funny voice.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Hosting a wild 'Monster Dance Party' on the table, making all our creations wiggle, shake, and shout silly words together.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What is your monster's name? Does it make a squeaky sound or a roaring sound when it wiggles?", "Try": "If a monster's wiggly leg falls off, make a dramatic 'Oh no!' sound, poke a deep finger hole into the clay together, and pinch it tight while saying '1, 2, 3, stick!'", "Watch": "Watch for children giggling and engaging in pretend play. Keep an eye out to make sure small googly eyes and pom-poms stay on the trays and out of mouths.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Wake Up & Splat!", "ChildrenDo": "Tickle, squeeze, and gently splat their clay on the tray to warm it up, making silly 'wake up' sounds.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead the stretching and squeezing with funny sound effects like 'streeeetch' and 'splat!'.", "DurationMinutes": 6}, {"Name": "The Magic Reveal", "ChildrenDo": "Lift up their surprise cups to discover the eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and feathers hidden underneath.", "TeacherDoes": "Gasp in excitement and encourage children to name the colorful treasures they found under their 'surprise hats'.", "DurationMinutes": 4}, {"Name": "Build a Goofy Friend", "ChildrenDo": "Press eyes, zig-zag legs, and feathers into their clay to build a silly monster face and body.", "TeacherDoes": "Circulate and make funny 'boop' and 'pop' noises as kids press items into the clay.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "The Monster Dance Party!", "ChildrenDo": "Bounce their finished monsters on the table dance floor, making them wiggle and make silly noises.", "TeacherDoes": "Play some upbeat, playful background music and narrate the funny dance moves the monsters are doing.", "DurationMinutes": 5}], "Safety": ["Ensure pipe cleaner ends are tucked or folded so they are not sharp.", "Actively supervise to prevent any mouthing of the small googly eyes or pom-poms."], "Closing": "Let's tuck our tired monsters into their trays for a nap. Let's whisper, 'Goodnight, silly monsters, thank you for dancing with us!'", "Extensions": ["Create a simple 'Monster Freeze Dance' game where children freeze their clay monsters when the music stops.", "Provide cardboard boxes as 'monster cars' to slide them around the room."], "Adaptations": ["For younger toddlers: Focus purely on poking big craft sticks and giant eyes into the clay to make 'peek-a-boo' faces.", "For older children: Challenge them to make a monster that can balance on one giant wiggly leg."], "Preparation": ["Roll the clay into round 'sleeping' balls.", "Bend several pipe cleaners into zig-zags and spirals.", "Hide the eyes, feathers, and pom-poms under plastic cups on each tray like secret surprise hats."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place a 'sleeping' ball of clay on each tray.", "Place the upside-down surprise cups next to the clay."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Create a 'Monster Dance Floor' by taping a bright sheet of paper in the center of the table.", "Set up individual trays for each child to contain the playdough splats."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Shh... look! Our clay balls are fast asleep! On the count of three, let's give them a gentle tickle and shout 'WAKE UP, CLAY!' One, two, three... WAKE UP!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child puts an eye in a weird place like the bottom of the clay, dramatically pretend the monster is looking at your toes and giggle together about its 'toe-vision'.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy high and full of pretend play. Use your voice to bring the clay to life—giggle when you touch it, make 'boop' sounds when adding eyes, and encourage children to talk to their monsters. Focus on the joy of creation rather than making a perfect figure.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:18:56.781161+00
3af934b8-2c5b-4cff-b2d3-b0080b8af840	a7409952-18ab-47e0-a7ab-a5b53426343f	9	The Wiggle-Wobble Clay Monster Party (Focus: Make It More Playful)	Children wake up sleeping lumps of clay with silly tickles and splats, then transform them into goofy, wiggly monster friends using shaky googly eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and fluffy tickle-feathers. Adapted with emphasis on: Make it more playful.	{"AgeMax": 3, "AgeMin": 2, "Create": "Pinch a goofy nose, press in tons of googly eyes, add bouncy zig-zag pipe cleaner legs, and give your monster a fluffy feather tail for tickling.", "Together": "Let's pat, squeeze, and gently splat our clay on the table! We'll tickle the clay to 'wake it up' and make funny yawning noises together.", "Challenge": "Can you make a super silly, wiggly clay monster that loves to dance and make funny noises? Challenge adaptation: Make it more playful.", "Materials": ["Brightly colored playdough or air-dry clay", "Assorted sizes of googly eyes", "Colorful pipe cleaners (some pre-bent into zig-zags)", "Fluffy craft feathers", "Soft mini pom-poms for silly noses", "Small plastic cups (to hide the craft parts under)"], "Reflection": "Show me your monster's favorite dance move! What silly sound does it make when you tickle its nose?", "ChildChoice": "Deciding how many eyes to stack, bending pipe cleaners into wild shapes, and inventing their monster's signature silly voice and dance move.", "Environment": "Indoor / Outdoor", "MagicMoment": "When a child tickles their finished monster's tummy with a feather and lets out a huge, shared giggle as they act out the monster's funny voice.", "GroupSizeMax": 10, "GroupSizeMin": 4, "Accomplishment": "Hosting a wild 'Monster Dance Party' on the table, making all our creations wiggle, shake, and shout silly words together.", "ExecutionGuide": {"Ask": "What is your monster's name? Does it make a squeaky sound or a roaring sound when it wiggles?", "Try": "If a monster's wiggly leg falls off, make a dramatic 'Oh no!' sound, poke a deep finger hole into the clay together, and pinch it tight while saying '1, 2, 3, stick!'", "Watch": "Watch for children giggling and engaging in pretend play. Keep an eye out to make sure small googly eyes and pom-poms stay on the trays and out of mouths.", "Phases": [{"Name": "Wake Up & Splat!", "ChildrenDo": "Tickle, squeeze, and gently splat their clay on the tray to warm it up, making silly 'wake up' sounds.", "TeacherDoes": "Lead the stretching and squeezing with funny sound effects like 'streeeetch' and 'splat!'.", "DurationMinutes": 6}, {"Name": "The Magic Reveal", "ChildrenDo": "Lift up their surprise cups to discover the eyes, wiggly pipe cleaners, and feathers hidden underneath.", "TeacherDoes": "Gasp in excitement and encourage children to name the colorful treasures they found under their 'surprise hats'.", "DurationMinutes": 4}, {"Name": "Build a Goofy Friend", "ChildrenDo": "Press eyes, zig-zag legs, and feathers into their clay to build a silly monster face and body.", "TeacherDoes": "Circulate and make funny 'boop' and 'pop' noises as kids press items into the clay.", "DurationMinutes": 10}, {"Name": "The Monster Dance Party!", "ChildrenDo": "Bounce their finished monsters on the table dance floor, making them wiggle and make silly noises.", "TeacherDoes": "Play some upbeat, playful background music and narrate the funny dance moves the monsters are doing.", "DurationMinutes": 5}], "Safety": ["Ensure pipe cleaner ends are tucked or folded so they are not sharp.", "Actively supervise to prevent any mouthing of the small googly eyes or pom-poms."], "Closing": "Let's tuck our tired monsters into their trays for a nap. Let's whisper, 'Goodnight, silly monsters, thank you for dancing with us!'", "Extensions": ["Create a simple 'Monster Freeze Dance' game where children freeze their clay monsters when the music stops.", "Provide cardboard boxes as 'monster cars' to slide them around the room."], "Adaptations": ["For younger toddlers: Focus purely on poking big craft sticks and giant eyes into the clay to make 'peek-a-boo' faces.", "For older children: Challenge them to make a monster that can balance on one giant wiggly leg."], "Preparation": ["Roll the clay into round 'sleeping' balls.", "Bend several pipe cleaners into zig-zags and spirals.", "Hide the eyes, feathers, and pom-poms under plastic cups on each tray like secret surprise hats."], "MaterialSetup": ["Place a 'sleeping' ball of clay on each tray.", "Place the upside-down surprise cups next to the clay."], "EnvironmentSetup": ["Create a 'Monster Dance Floor' by taping a bright sheet of paper in the center of the table.", "Set up individual trays for each child to contain the playdough splats."], "TeacherIntroduction": "Shh... look! Our clay balls are fast asleep! On the count of three, let's give them a gentle tickle and shout 'WAKE UP, CLAY!' One, two, three... WAKE UP!", "LittleMomentOpportunity": "When a child puts an eye in a weird place like the bottom of the clay, dramatically pretend the monster is looking at your toes and giggle together about its 'toe-vision'.", "SuggestedDurationMinutes": 25}, "TeacherGuidance": "Keep the energy high and full of pretend play. Use your voice to bring the clay to life—giggle when you touch it, make 'boop' sounds when adding eyes, and encourage children to talk to their monsters. Focus on the joy of creation rather than making a perfect figure.", "DevelopmentalGoals": []}	Draft Saved	a824798d-e5f4-444d-9fa6-76491e2be21d	2026-10-15 00:19:44.111307+00
