raft sticks and giant eyes into the clay to make \\u0027peek-a-boo\\u0027 faces.","For older children: Challenge them to make a monster that can balance on one giant wiggly leg."],"Preparation":["Roll the clay into round \\u0027sleeping\\u0027 balls.","Bend several pipe cleaners into zig-zags and spirals.","Hide the eyes, feathers, and pom-poms under plastic cups on each tray like secret surprise hats."],"MaterialSetup":["Place a \\u0027sleeping\\u0027 ball of clay on each tray.","Place the upside-down surprise cups next to the clay."],"EnvironmentSetup":["Create a \\u0027Monster Dance Floor\\u0027 by taping a bright sheet of paper in the center of the table.","Set up individual trays for each child to contain the playdough splats."],"TeacherIntroduction":"Shh... look! Our clay balls are fast asleep! On the count of three, let\\u0027s give them a gentle tickle and shout \\u0027WAKE UP, CLAY!\\u0027 One, two, three... WAKE UP!","LittleMomentOpportunity":"When a child puts an eye in a weird place like the bottom of the clay, dramatically pretend the monster is looking at your toes and giggle together about its \\u0027toe-vision\\u0027.","SuggestedDurationMinutes":25},"TeacherGuidance":"Keep the energy high and full of pretend play. Use your voice to bring the clay to life\\u2014giggle when you touch it, make \\u0027boop\\u0027 sounds when adding eyes, and encourage children to talk to their monsters. Focus on the joy of creation rather than making a perfect figure.","DevelopmentalGoals":[]}	Initial curated release from organization submission	50000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001	2026-10-15 21:59:38.128167+00	t
669d9a46-2007-4562-a0c3-d0e5b6c2da16	009ed10c-1cb3-4275-987c-d039b6658272	1	Exploring Playing With Colorful Dough: Creative Tactile Studio (Focus: Make It More Playful)	Children engage in open-ended creative construction and tactile exploration centered on Playing with colorful dough. Adapted with emphasis on: Make it more playful.	{"AgeMax":5,"AgeMin":3,"Create":"A tangible collaborative model or collage.","Together":"Children share building materials and describe what they are making to their peers.","Challenge":"Can we design and build a display inspired by Playing with colorful dough? Challenge adaptation: Make it more playful.","Materials":["Cardstock","Washable craft adhesive","Wooden craft sticks","Textured paper scraps"],"Reflection":"What was your favorite texture or tool you used while exploring Playing with colorful dough?","Environment":"Indoor / Outdoor","MagicMoment":"Noticing how different materials join together to form a new creation.","GroupSizeMax":10,"GroupSizeMin":4,"Accomplishment":"Presenting their completed project during reflection circle.","ExecutionGuide":{"Ask":"How does that piece feel when you touch it?","Try":"Try connecting two different shapes together.","Watch":"Observe hand-eye coordination and peer turn-taking.","Phases":[{"Name":"Welcome \\u0026 Material Touch","ChildrenDo":"Feel materials and select initial pieces","TeacherDoes":"Demonstrates gentle handling and asks open questions","DurationMinutes":5},{"Name":"Hands-On Creation","ChildrenDo":"Assemble and combine elements","TeacherDoes":"Circulates, providing positive reinforcement","DurationMinutes":15},{"Name":"Sharing \\u0026 Clean-Up","ChildrenDo":"Place finished crafts on drying rack and tidy up","TeacherDoes":"Leads a gentle closing transition","DurationMinutes":5}],"Safety":["Ensure only child-safe non-toxic adhesive is used","Remind children that materials remain on the table"],"Closing":"Gather on the carpet for a quick proud celebration of everyone\\u0027s work.","Ex